  "body": "## v0.21.10 - 2026-09-30\n\n### Added\n- Add opt-in engine CPU and memory telemetry from cgroup v2 by @matipan in https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/14285 \\\n  Enable `telemetry.resourceMetrics` in `/etc/dagger/engine.json` and set\n  `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_METRICS_ENDPOINT` in the engine environment. The new\n  resource metrics use a separate OTLP export path. Client telemetry is\n  unchanged, and engine resource telemetry is disabled by default.\n\n### Fixed\n- `Container.withServiceBinding` now works for a service whose custom hostname by @Zaba505 in https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/13751 \\\n  was scoped to another module's DNS domain. A service created with\n  `withHostname` is namespaced into the domain of whichever module first starts\n  it, so a module that started its own service before handing it to a caller\n  left that caller's `withExec` failing at hosts-file setup with\n  `lookup <hostname> ... no such host`. Binding the service explicitly now\n  resolves it by the name it actually registered under. Reaching a module's\n  custom hostname by bare DNS remains scoped to that module.\n- `dag.CurrentModule().Source()` and `dag.CurrentModule().GeneratedContextDirectory()` by @marcosnils in https://github.com/dagger/dagger/pull/ \\\n  no longer re-resolve the running module's declared dependencies. The module's\n  already-loaded dependency DAG is reused for codegen, so these APIs now work in\n  modules whose dependencies point at private git repositories even when the\n  runtime caller does not hold the author's credentials.\n\n### Contributors\nSpecial thanks to our external contributors this release!\n- @Zaba505\n\n### What to do next?\n- Read the [documentation](https://docs.dagger.io)\n- Join our [Discord server](https://discord.gg/dagger-io)\n- Follow us on [Twitter](https://twitter.com/dagger_io)\n\n",
  "mentions_count": 3
}
